---
import DocsLayout from '../../layouts/docs-layout.astro';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
---

<DocsLayout
  title="Introduction"
  page="introduction"
  nav={[
    {
      href: '#intro',
      label: 'Introduction',
    },
    {
      href: '#why',
      label: 'Why MiniKit',
    },
    {
      href: '#advantages',
      label: 'Advantages',
    },
    {
      href: '#start',
      label: 'Start Now',
    },
  ]}
>
  <section id="intro">
    <h1>Introduction</h1>

    <p class="lead">
      MiniKit is a lightweight, HTML-first design system foundation for building modern web
      interfaces.
    </p>

    <p>
      It provides thoughtfully designed styles for typography, forms, buttons, layouts, and common
      UI components while staying close to native HTML. MiniKit embraces progressive enhancement,
      using CSS for presentation and minimal JavaScript only where interaction is required. The
      result is a fast, accessible, and framework-agnostic toolkit that works with any stack.
      Designed with simplicity in mind, MiniKit helps developers build consistent interfaces without
      the complexity, abstraction, or overhead commonly found in larger UI frameworks.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="why">
    <h2>Why MiniKit?</h2>

    <p>
      Many UI frameworks trade simplicity for abstraction, requiring custom APIs, heavy JavaScript
      runtimes, or complex build tooling. MiniKit takes a different approach by enhancing the
      platform rather than replacing it. By building on semantic HTML and modern browser features,
      MiniKit allows developers to create interfaces using familiar markup while benefiting from
      accessible components and sensible defaults. Its small footprint, minimal JavaScript, and
      framework-agnostic design make it a practical choice for projects that value performance,
      maintainability, and long-term simplicity.
    </p>

    <p>
      Whether you're building a static website, documentation site, internal tool, or full web
      application, MiniKit provides a solid foundation without getting in your way.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="advantages">
    <h2>Advantages of MiniKit</h2>

    <div class="advantages-grid">
      <article>
        <h3>HTML First</h3>

        <p>Build interfaces using semantic HTML instead of framework-specific abstractions.</p>
      </article>

      <article>
        <h3>Tiny Bundle</h3>

        <p>Approximately 6.4 KB of CSS and 1.8 KB of JavaScript.</p>
      </article>

      <article>
        <h3>Accessible</h3>

        <p>Components include keyboard support and sensible accessibility defaults.</p>
      </article>

      <article>
        <h3>Framework Agnostic</h3>

        <p>Works with Astro, React, Vue, HTMX, Alpine.js, or plain HTML.</p>
      </article>

      <article>
        <h3>Zero Dependencies</h3>

        <p>No runtime dependencies and no unnecessary complexity.</p>
      </article>

      <article>
        <h3>Customizable</h3>

        <p>Easily adapt colors, spacing, typography, and component styles.</p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="start">
    <h2>Start Now</h2>

    <p>Ready to start building with MiniKit?</p>

    <div class="start-grid">
      <a href={`${base}installation/`} data-card>
        <h3>Installation</h3>

        <p>Add MiniKit to your project using npm or a CDN.</p>
      </a>

      <a href={`${base}customization/how/`} data-card>
        <h3>Customization</h3>

        <p>Learn how to customize MiniKit using CSS variables.</p>
      </a>

      <a href={`${base}components/button/`} data-card>
        <h3>Components</h3>

        <p>Explore the component library and usage examples.</p>
      </a>
    </div>
  </section>
</DocsLayout>
